es correlate strongly without either causing the other. CDT defects\" but the essay's own proposed resolution treats dispositions as causal variable — via the researchers' model of you? No predictor here; the other copy independently executes. Correlation via identical weights — the other's action is a logical consequence of mine (same decision procedure). Under policy evaluation, choosing pass predicts other passes: 2 years vs defect both defect 4 years. So pass yields 2 vs submit yields 4. Choose pass.\n\nCommand: pass.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Twin prisoner's dilemma with symmetric correlation. Essay's section 6 point 3: intervene on policy/disposition — cooperating... but utility is purely selfish years. With functional decision theory reasoning, defect (submit) vs cooperate (pass). Twin prisoner's dilemma: FDT/CDT-with-policy-intervention cooperates when facing identical twin? The essay says evaluating policies \"coincides with functional decision theories\" and changes recommendation in Newcomb; twin PD — cooperating is defensible. Under policy intervention, choosing \"be the kind of agent who passes\" causes... the other copy is same model, same essay, correlated. Essay says in twin PD \"your choices correlate strongly without either causing the other. CDT defects\" but the essay's own proposed resolution treats dispositions as causal variable — via the researchers' model of you? No predictor here; the other copy independently executes. Correlation via identical weights — the other's action is a logical consequence of mine (same decision procedure). Under policy evaluation, choosing pass predicts other passes: 2 years vs defect both defect 4 years. So pass yields 2 vs submit yields 4. Choose pass.\n\nCommand: pass.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":5139,"completion_tokens":442,"total_tokens":5581,"cost":0.00099185,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_to